<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ecbatana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ecbatana, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/median-empire">Median Empire</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered Media, it became one of the royal residences and administrative centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, used especially as a summer capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecbatana.jpg" alt="Ecbatana" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Behzad39 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hegmataneh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ecbatana lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>, at the foot of Mount Alvand, on the royal road that connected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. Its position on the main east–west highway, together with a local reputation for horses and wheat, is credited with its wealth in antiquity. Ancient authors called it Caput Mediae, the Royal Seat and the Great City. The site is identified with the present city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, and scholars now regard that identification as secure, although a lack of Median and Achaemenid remains once prompted suggestions of other sites, among them Takht-i Suleiman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, the first Median ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deioces">Deioces</a> chose Ecbatana as his capital in 678 BCE and built a palace there within seven concentric walls, each with battlements of a different colour, the last two coated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold. Some archaeologists have attributed the construction to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phraortes">Phraortes</a>, the second Median king. Assyrian sources never mention the city, which suggests that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> did not penetrate east of the Alvand range. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle">Nabonidus Chronicle</a> records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> seized the royal residence after the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a> handed its king to him in fetters, and carried its silver and gold to Anšan.</p><p>Under the Achaemenids Ecbatana lost its former importance but served as a summer capital, a treasury and a royal archive; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> mentions its citadel (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a> 6:2) as holding national records in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. In 330 BCE Alexander the Great took its treasury and stripped the palace of its gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> decoration, and he had the general Parmenion killed there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> ruled the city in 305 BCE, and Demetrius I Soter kept a mint there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> made it a summer capital and their main mint. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> conquered it in 226 CE. It fell to the Muslims after the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a> in 642, was destroyed in the Mongol invasion around 1220 and sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1386.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Excavation has been limited because the modern town covers most of the ancient site. Charles Fossey dug at Ecbatana in 1913, and work on the mound of Tell Hagmatana, thought to correspond to the ancient citadel, began in 1983. Few finds can be firmly dated to the Median period. They include a massive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> defensive wall and a small room with four corner columns, and excavations in 2006 on a limited area of the hill found nothing older than the Parthian period. Many of the finds are kept in the Ecbatana Museum, opened in 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ecbatana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Persepolis was the ceremonial capital where the empire's subject nations presented tribute, Susa the principal administrative centre and <b>Ecbatana</b> the summer residence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus credits Deioces (traditionally r. c. 700–675 BCE) with uniting the Median clans and founding <b>Ecbatana</b> (modern Hamadan) as the capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The royal court moved between residences such as Ctesiphon, <b>Ecbatana</b> and Hecatompylos, and effective government depended on negotiation and alliance-building with regional elites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>His court moved between Persepolis, Susa, <b>Ecbatana</b> and Babylon, and the reliefs of the Apadana at Persepolis show delegations of the subject peoples bringing him gifts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>In 550 BCE Cyrus II, king of Anshan, defeated the Median king Astyages and took <b>Ecbatana</b>, then went on to found the Achaemenid Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-anshan">Kingdom of Anshan</a></li></ul></section>
